page=namespace sect=6 summary="namespace files: scripts that build a namespace"
    src=lib/vx-ns/newns.c,lib/vx-ns/ns.c,lib/vx-ns/spawn.c,servers/svcd/svcd.c
    keys=newns,mount,bind,unmount,union,template

# SYNOPSIS

```text
mount [-abcC] service old [spec]
bind [-abcC] new old
unmount [new] old
cd dir
clear
. file
```

# DESCRIPTION

A namespace file describes how to build a namespace, one operation to a line, as Plan 9's do.
The namespace templates are in `/lib/ns`: a service manifest's `ns=<name>` names `/lib/ns/<name>` (see svc(6)), and svcd(8) applies it when it starts the service.
ns(1) prints a namespace as a namespace file, procfs(4) serves one as `/proc/<pid>/ns`, and nsd(4) keeps each namespace group's as one, which every member replays.
`lib/vx-ns` parses them, as Plan 9's `newns` does, and the program applying a file decides what each operation does.
A namespace file is a script, not data, so it is not ndb(6).

Words are separated by spaces and tabs.
A single quote starts or ends a quoted part of a word, which may hold spaces, tabs, `$` and `#`; inside one, `''` is a quote, and `a'b c'd` is one word, `ab cd`.
`''` alone is an empty word.
Outside quotes, `$<name>` is replaced by the value of the variable <name>, which ends at a space, a tab, a `/`, a `$` or a quote; a variable with no value is empty.
A line whose first word starts with `#` is a comment, and a blank line is ignored.
A `#` anywhere else is part of a word, so a comment cannot follow an operation on its line.
A line has at most six words, at most 1024 bytes of them once expanded.

An operation's first word names it.
The second word of a `mount` or a `bind`, if it starts with `-` and has letters after it, holds its flags, all in that one word:

: `-a`
  Add after: the new directory joins the union at <old>, after what is there.
: `-b`
  Add before: it joins the union before what is there.
: `-c`
  Create: a file created in a union of more than one member is created in the first member with `-c`, as 9front's `createdir` chooses, and such a union with none refuses a create.
: `-C`
  Plan 9's caching; accepted, and ignored.

With neither `-a` nor `-b`, the new directory replaces the union at <old>; with both, the line is refused.

The operations are:

: `mount` `[-abcC]` <service> <old> [<spec>]
  Mounts the file server <service> on <old>, attaching to the tree <spec> names, the attach name, or to its root without one.
  What a <service> is depends on who applies the file: `/srv/<name>` is a service by its post's name; `tcp!<host>!<port>` or `9p://<host>:<port>` is a 9P server reached over TCP.
: `bind` `[-abcC]` <new> <old>
  Makes <old> show what <new> names now.
  A union at <new> is copied whole, its members in order; a union bound onto itself is refused.
: `unmount` [<new>] <old>
  Removes what was mounted or bound from <new> at <old>, or everything at <old> without <new>.
  For a mount, <new> is the service as `mount` named it, `/srv/tmpfs`.
: `cd` <dir>
  Changes the working directory to <dir>.
: `clear`
  Empties the namespace, as Plan 9's `rfork(RFCNAMEG)`.
: `.` <file>
  Runs the namespace file <file>, which must be in the namespace being built.

Any other first word, a wrong number of words, a flag not listed above and an unclosed quote are errors, reported with the line.

<old> and <new> are absolute paths.
Each is cleaned before use, so `..` never climbs out of a bind, and each component must be UTF-8 with no control character (see utf(6)); a path is at most 256 bytes, a service's name 64.
A mount point is found by the identity of the directory it was made on, not by the path it was made at, so a mount shows through every name that reaches that directory (ADR-0009).
A namespace holds at most 32 mount points, 8 members to a union and 16 servers.

@node=appliers title="Who applies them"

svcd(8) reads a template straight from the boot image, as `lib/ns/<name>`.
It does only `mount` of a post and `bind`: each line becomes one of the mount and bind records of the service's spawn message, where the manifest's `ns=` record stands, and the service's runtime replays them in order before `vx_main` runs.
A template with any other line, or a mount of a post that cannot be made, is refused, and the service is not started.
svcd has no variables, so a `$<name>` is always empty.

A member of a namespace group replays the group's text whenever it has changed: a `mount` through a connection the member already has from <service>, through nsd(4)'s connector for a `/srv/` name, or by dialing an address; `bind` and `unmount` as written.
It skips `cd`, `clear` and `.`.
A line that fails is reported, `vx-ns: cannot replay line <n> of the namespace group's:` and why, and the lines after it are still replayed.

ns(1), `/proc/<pid>/ns` and the text nsd(4) keeps are written by `vx_ns_print`.
It writes only `mount` and `bind` lines, one for each member of each union in the order they were added, since a later one may need a path an earlier one made.
The first member of a union to be added has no flag; each later one has `-b` if it comes before every member added so far and `-a` otherwise; and a member that takes creates has `-c` too.
A word holding a space, a tab, a quote, `$` or `#`, or an empty one, is quoted.

rc(1)'s builtins `bind`, `mount` and `unmount` take the same words, and rc quotes as this format does, so ns(1)'s output can be run by `rc` as well.

# EXAMPLES

The POSIX template, `boot/lib/ns/posix`, which a manifest's `ns=posix` gives a service; its comment is left out:

```text
mount /srv/bootfs /
bind -a /boot/bin /bin
bind -b /boot/bin/posix /bin
mount -c /srv/tmpfs /tmp
mount /srv/null /dev
mount -a /srv/ptyd /dev
mount /srv/proc /proc
mount /srv/net /net
mount /srv/sys /sys
```

A manifest that uses it, `tests/user/ctest.ndb`, cut short:

```ndb
service=ctest program=/boot/bin/ctest console entropy
arg=one
arg="two words"
env=GREETING=hello
ns=posix
connect=nsd
```

A mount, printed again by ns(1):

```rc
% mount /srv/bootfs /n; ls /n; ns | tail -1
adm  bin  boot  dev  dist  lib  n  net  proc  rc  srv  sys  tmp
mount /srv/bootfs /n
```

# FILES

: `/lib/ns/*`
  The namespace templates.
: `/lib/ns/posix`
  The namespace a POSIX program expects.
: `/proc/<pid>/ns`
  The text of the namespace group process <pid> is in.

# SEE ALSO

ns(1), rc(1), nsd(4), procfs(4), svc(6), svcd(8), utf(6); ADR-0009 and docs/02 §2 in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

svcd(8) reports `svcd: <service>: no namespace template <name>` for a template not in the boot image, `svcd: <service>: namespace template <name>: no operation on line <n>` for a line it cannot parse, and `svcd: <service>: namespace template <name>: cannot do line <n>` for one it does not apply.

# BUGS

No program applies `cd`, `clear` or `.` yet: svcd refuses a template holding one, and a group's replay skips them.

There is no `newns` command, and nothing gives a namespace file variables, so `$<name>` is always empty.

Flags must be one word: in `mount -a -c /srv/tmpfs /tmp`, the `-c` is taken as the service, and the line mounts the wrong thing or is refused.

Plan 9's `chdev` is not an operation, since VectraOS has no kernel devices to restrict.
